+"""Balanced-table binary CSP generator grounded in arXiv:0911.0801.
+
+The paper defines CSP instances as variables, a common domain, and explicitly
+listed allowed tuples (Definition 2.1), then proves an ETH lower bound for
+bounded-arity classes of unbounded width (Theorems 1.2 and 7.1).  This module
+inverse-generates a satisfying assignment and binary relation tables around it.
+Every table is biregular, so the planted value has exactly the same one-edge
+support statistics as every decoy value.
+
+Generation never solves an instance.  Verification is an exact scan of table
+membership.  The module is deterministic in (n, seed, params), uses only the
+standard library, performs no file or network I/O, and is silent on import.
+"""
+
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import hashlib
+import itertools
+import json
+import math
+import os
+import random
+import re
+import time
+from typing import Any
+
+
+TRACK = "A"
+
+PROBLEM_PROFILE = {
+    "native_domain": "logic",
+    "object_regime": "finite_discrete",
+    "computational_core": "csp_sat",
+    "certificate_form": "integer_tuple",
+    "native_objects": [
+        "finite-domain CSP variables",
+        "binary constraint relations represented by exact row bitsets",
+    ],
+    "verification_operations": [
+        "integer range comparison",
+        "exact allowed-tuple membership by bit lookup",
+    ],
+    "domain_essentiality": "native",
+    "reduction_kind": "none",
+    "reduction": None,
+    "reduction_source": "paper_central",
+    "intuition_type": "constraint propagation",
+    "intuition_description": (
+        "Only cycle consistency distinguishes the satisfying values because "
+        "every individual relation has identical row and column degrees; "
+        "without propagating coupled domains, local frequencies give no clue."
+    ),
+    "hardness_basis": (
+        "Track A: Theorem 1.2 covers bounded-arity CSP classes of unbounded "
+        "treewidth (and Theorem 7.1 strengthens this using unbounded submodular "
+        "width); shipping uses binary constraints on a sparse, growing-width "
+        "random-core regime with q=16 and balanced density 1/2, for which no "
+        "efficient algorithm is known on this generated distribution, and G5 "
+        "reports the measured MAC/DPLL node and wall-clock cost."
+    ),
+    "max_answer_tokens": 0,  # replaced after the shipping preset is measured
+}
+
+NATIVE = {
+    "domain": PROBLEM_PROFILE["native_domain"],
+    "core": PROBLEM_PROFILE["computational_core"],
+    "objects": PROBLEM_PROFILE["native_objects"],
+    "intuition": (
+        PROBLEM_PROFILE["intuition_type"] + ": "
+        + PROBLEM_PROFILE["intuition_description"]
+    ),
+    "reduction": PROBLEM_PROFILE["reduction"],
+}
+
+DIFFICULTY = {
+    "demo": {"n": 6, "domain_size": 4, "relation_degree": 2,
+             "edge_multiplier": 2},
+    "easy": {"n": 72, "domain_size": 16, "relation_degree": 8,
+             "edge_multiplier": 5},
+    "medium": {"n": 96, "domain_size": 16, "relation_degree": 8,
+               "edge_multiplier": 5},
+    "hard": {"n": 128, "domain_size": 16, "relation_degree": 8,
+             "edge_multiplier": 5},
+}
+SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY = "easy"
+
+STRUCTURAL_HINT = (
+    "The satisfying values are distinguished only by consistency around cycles; "
+    "each individual relation has perfectly equal row and column degrees."
+)
+PLACEBO_HINT = (
+    "The satisfying values should be checked carefully across the displayed "
+    "constraints; each relation uses a compact row-bitset representation."
+)
+
+CERTIFICATE_LANGUAGE = {
+    "description": (
+        "A JSON array of exactly n integers, one value in 0..q-1 for each "
+        "variable in index order; q <= 32 and n <= 512."
+    ),
+    "bounds": {
+        "max_variables": 512,
+        "max_domain_size": 32,
+        "min_value": 0,
+        "max_value": 31,
+    },
+}
+
+NOTES = r"""
+Paper grounding and Step 0.  Definition 2.1 fixes the exact object used here:
+a CSP instance is (V,D,C), every constraint is a scope plus an allowed relation,
+and a solution is a function V -> D whose tuple belongs to every relation.  The
+paper explicitly assumes relations are represented by listing allowed tuples.
+For a fixed binary domain, the row-bitsets rendered here are merely that same
+table representation with each row's listed set encoded losslessly as bits.
+
+The easy and hard regimes.  Section 4, especially Theorem 4.1, gives an FPT
+algorithm for bounded submodular width by consistency enforcement, uniform
+splitting, and adaptive tree decompositions.  Theorem 1.1 says that for bounded
+arity, bounded treewidth is the tractable structural boundary (subject to the
+stated parameterized-complexity assumption); Theorem 1.2 gives the sharper ETH
+lower bound.  Theorem 7.1 gives the paper's unbounded-submodular-width ETH lower
+bound.  Our sparse graph core grows with n and is not a bounded-width family.
+The theorem is worst-case evidence only: it does not prove this inverse-planted
+distribution hard.  The distributional claim is therefore limited to the
+explicit adversary measurements in selftest and the external oracle transcript.
+
+What produces the certificate.  The assignment is sampled first.  For every
+constraint, independent random row and column permutations hide a circulant
+biregular relation, and the permitted shifts include the planted pair.  Thus the
+certificate is known before the instance exists.  No CSP, SAT, treewidth,
+consistency, or search algorithm runs during generation.
+
+Attack resistance.  Every row and every column of every q-by-q relation has
+exactly relation_degree allowed tuples, so support-count and degree outliers tie
+on every value.  Variable order and constraint order are randomized by the seed.
+The greedy attack uses assigned-neighbor compatibility, min-conflicts uses local
+repair, and loopy belief propagation tests whether small biases amplify through
+cycles.  The domain-standard attack is exact DPLL with maintained arc
+consistency and a measured node cap.  Plants and decoys are not sampled as two
+different tuple populations: after marginalizing over the uniformly sampled
+plant value, every value label has the same local distribution.
+""".strip()
